'''Koreasat 6''' is a South Korean [[communications satellite]] operated by [[Koreasat]]. Koreasat 6 is the second satellite launched by operator [[KT Corporation|KT]] (Korea Telecom) Corporation which weighed about 2750&nbsp;kg (6060 lb) at the launching time.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nasaspaceflight.com/2010/12/live-ariane-5-eca-end-year-koreasat-6-and-hispasat-1e/|title=Ariane 5 ECA ends 2010 by launching Koreasat 6 and Hispasat 1E|date=28 December 2010 }}</ref> This [[satellite]] [[Orbital mechanics|orbital]] position at 116 degrees East was changed to 160 degrees East in 2025 when contracted by [[Optus]] to provide broadcasting services to [[Sky (New Zealand)]].<ref> {{Cite web |date=2025-03-16 |title=Sky TV's new satellite placed into orbit - here's when affected customers should have full TV reception again |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/media-insider/media-insider-sky-tvs-new-satellite-placed-into-orbit-with-positive-early-results/U32CRITFI5HELOQ64YBYZAAY74/ |access-date=2025-03-19 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ}} </ref> The new Koreasat 6A launched in 2024 replacing Koreasat 6 in its old orbital position of 116 degrees East.<ref> {{Cite web |date=2024-11-12 |title=Koreasat 6A communications satellite successfully launched {{!}} Thales Alenia Space |url=https://www.thalesaleniaspace.com/en/press-releases/koreasat-6a-communications-satellite-successfully-launched |access-date=2025-03-19 |website=www.thalesaleniaspace.com |language=en}} </ref>

